      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182161",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following sequence that diverges to $\u221e$:\n\n$a_{n} = 5\\sqrt{n}, n \\ge 1$\n\nLet $M > 0$. We wish to find a natural number $N(M)$ such that for all $n \\ge N$, we have $a_{n} > M$.\n\nComplete the reasoning below, and hence find a suitable $N$ that satisfies this condition.\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality $a_{n} > M$ for $n$:\n\n$M$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$\\sqrt{M}$ {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\n\nTherefore, $5\\sqrt{n}$\n{{field-5}}\nfor all natural numbers $n \\ge N$."
    },
